{"name":"Sunstone","hex":"#c7887f","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"sunstone","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A translucent form of feldspar having flakes of hematite, used as an ornamental stone.","A stone, possibly Icelandic spar, said in late medieval sources to have been used by navigators in the Viking Age and later to find the location of the sun in an overcast sky, possibly via polarization of light."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/sunstone"}],"entries":[{"title":"Sunstone","blocks":[{"text":"Sunstone is a microcline or oligoclase feldspar, which when viewed from certain directions exhibits a spangled appearance. It has been found in Southern Norway, Sweden, various localities in the United States, and on some beaches along the midcoast of South Australia."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/fc/Sunstone1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sunstone","wikidata":"Q2729026"},{"title":"Sunstone (medieval)","blocks":[{"text":"The sunstone is a type of mineral attested in several 13th–14th-century written sources in Iceland, one of which describes its use to locate the Sun in a completely overcast sky. Sunstones are also mentioned in the inventories of several churches and one monastery in 14th–15th-century Iceland and Germany."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/14/Silfurberg.jpg/330px-Silfurberg.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sunstone_(medieval)","wikidata":"Q1895983"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/81/Sunstone2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 1x","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/81/Sunstone2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","alt":"Unpolished sunstone","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASunstone2.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4c/Sunstone_6.jpg/500px-Sunstone_6.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4c/Sunstone_6.jpg/1280px-Sunstone_6.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4c/Sunstone_6.jpg/500px-Sunstone_6.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A tumbled sunstone","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASunstone_6.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/65/Labradoriteoregonsunstone.jpg/330px-Labradoriteoregonsunstone.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/65/Labradoriteoregonsunstone.jpg/330px-Labradoriteoregonsunstone.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Various gem colors of Oregon sunstone","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALabradoriteoregonsunstone.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8a/Sunstone_in_practice.png/500px-Sunstone_in_practice.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8a/Sunstone_in_practice.png/1280px-Sunstone_in_practice.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8a/Sunstone_in_practice.png/500px-Sunstone_in_practice.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Demonstration of a sunstone used to locate the sun's position by comparing the intensity of the ordinary and extraordinary images of a black dot marked on the crystal's surface opposite the observer.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASunstone_in_practice.png"}],"sounds":{}}}